ksf patch to lsfg-vk (NOT upstream) - Context::acquireAt(timestamp)

Against lsfg-vk commit fd8c317159ff5cc363cffa0f4d1e9568ceadf9dc (LSFG_COMMIT in
install.sh), which applies it with `patch -p1` in the lsfg-vk source tree.

Why: upstream acquire() always generates frame `index` of `total` at the evenly
spaced point (index + 1) / (total + 1) between two source frames, so the output
can only be a whole multiple of the input frame rate. vidinterp-gpu --fps makes
any rate (25 fps -> 120 fps for a 120 Hz screen, 4.8x) and needs each frame at
its own point, e.g. 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 for one pair and 0.0, 0.2, ... for the
next. LSFG's shaders already read that point from a uniform (the "timestamp"),
so the patch only lets the caller choose it:
  - lsfgvk.hpp/.cpp: Context::acquireAt(float timestamp, bool unsignaled);
    acquire() is unchanged (it calls acquireAt with -1 = the even spacing).
  - pipeline.hpp/.cpp: buildTransCmdbuf takes the timestamp; an explicit one
    is never cached (the same index/total means different timestamps from one
    pair to the next) and gets keys of its own (bit 63).

If lsfg-vk is ever updated (LSFG_COMMIT bumped): install.sh stops when this
no longer applies. Port it by hand - find where the transition command buffer
writes UniformBuffer.timestamp and let the caller pass it - and rebuild;
vidinterp_gpu.cpp does not compile without acquireAt(). Check the result is
unchanged for whole multiples (acquireAt(j / m) == the old acquire()).

lsfg-vk is CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0: modifying it for our own use is fine, but the
patched library must not be redistributed.

diff -ruN a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/include/lsfg-vk/lsfgvk.hpp b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/include/lsfg-vk/lsfgvk.hpp
--- a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/include/lsfg-vk/lsfgvk.hpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.789990000 +0200
+++ b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/include/lsfg-vk/lsfgvk.hpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.839785300 +0200
@@ -206,6 +206,20 @@
         void acquire(bool unsignaled = false);
 
         ///
+        /// Acquire a generated image at an explicit point between the two source
+        /// frames (0 = the previous one, 1 = the current one), instead of the even
+        /// spacing acquire() uses ((index + 1) / (total + 1)). Lets the caller
+        /// produce any output frame rate, not only whole multiples of the input.
+        ///
+        /// [ksf patch, vision/vidinterp/lsfg-vk-explicit-timestamp.patch - not upstream]
+        ///
+        /// @param timestamp Position between the source frames, in (0, 1)
+        /// @param unsignaled Do not wait for any timeline semaphores
+        /// @throws std::runtime_error on failure
+        ///
+        void acquireAt(float timestamp, bool unsignaled = false);
+
+        ///
         /// Wait for the dispatch to complete. This should only be
         /// called after the last acquire() and will block until the
         /// last frame is generated.
diff -ruN a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/lsfgvk.cpp b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/lsfgvk.cpp
--- a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/lsfgvk.cpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.790989000 +0200
+++ b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/lsfgvk.cpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.833266500 +0200
@@ -231,18 +231,27 @@
 }
 
 void Context::acquire(bool unsignaled) {
+    this->acquireAt(-1.0F, unsignaled); // [ksf patch] negative = the even spacing
+}
+
+// [ksf patch] acquire() with the timestamp given by the caller; see lsfgvk.hpp.
+void Context::acquireAt(float timestamp, bool unsignaled) {
     auto& ctx{*this->m_impl};
     const auto& vk{ctx.instance.get().vk};
 
     if (ctx.total == 0 || ctx.index >= ctx.total) {
         throw std::runtime_error("No more frames to acquire");
     }
+    if (timestamp >= 1.0F) {
+        throw std::runtime_error("Timestamp must lie between the two source frames");
+    }
 
     // Build transition command buffer to transition the timestamp
     const auto& transCmdbuf{ctx.pipeline.buildTransCmdbuf(
         *vk.dld, *vk.device,
         ctx.iteration,
-        ctx.index, ctx.total
+        ctx.index, ctx.total,
+        timestamp
     )};
 
     auto& sync{ctx.syncSemaphore};
diff -ruN a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/pipeline.cpp b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/pipeline.cpp
--- a/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/pipeline.cpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.794652300 +0200
+++ b/lsfg-vk-pipeline/src/pipeline.cpp	2026-09-25 12:40:53.835777900 +0200
@@ -838,10 +838,16 @@
     const vk::Device& device,
     uint32_t iteration,
     uint32_t index,
-    uint32_t total
+    uint32_t total,
+    float timestamp
 ) {
-    const bool persist{total > 8};
-    const uint64_t key{persist ? ((static_cast<uint64_t>(index) << 32) | total) : index};
+    // [ksf patch] An explicit timestamp is never cached (the same index and total
+    // stand for different timestamps from one pair to the next) and has keys of
+    // its own, so it cannot free a cached buffer that is still in use.
+    const bool explicitTimestamp{timestamp >= 0.0F};
+    const bool persist{!explicitTimestamp && total > 8};
+    const uint64_t key{explicitTimestamp ? ((uint64_t{1} << 63) | index)
+        : persist ? ((static_cast<uint64_t>(index) << 32) | total) : index};
 
     if (persist && this->m_transCmdbufs.contains(key))
         return *this->m_transCmdbufs.at(key);
@@ -872,7 +878,8 @@
     }, dld);
 
     const UniformBuffer buf{
-        .timestamp = static_cast<float>(index + 1) / static_cast<float>(total + 1),
+        .timestamp = explicitTimestamp ? timestamp
+            : static_cast<float>(index + 1) / static_cast<float>(total + 1),
         .iteration = iteration
     };
     cmdbuf->updateBuffer(
